<!-- <STRONG>WHY SCALE-UP STAGES MATTER IN AEROSOLS</STRONG> -->
<section class="section" id="why-scale-up-stages-matter-in-aerosols">
  <h2><strong>Why Scale-Up Stages Matter in Aerosols</strong></h2>
  <p>Aerosol products require a structured and controlled scale-up process because propellant behavior, valve
            mechanics, pressure stability, and formulation–hardware interaction change significantly with batch size.
            The transition from lab to pilot scale reduces technical uncertainty, while moving from pilot to commercial
            reduces operational and financial risk. Companies that treat all stages as identical often face spray
            failures, leakage, foaming issues, and inconsistent discharge rates during commercial production.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Prevents unexpected pressure deviation during filling</li>
            <li>Ensures proper valve–formulation compatibility</li>
            <li>Identifies propellant solubility and dispersion issues</li>
            <li>Allows stability validation under real storage conditions</li>
        </ul>
</section>

<!-- <STRONG>R&D FORMULATION FOR AEROSOL PRODUCTS</STRONG> -->
<section class="section" id="rd-formulation-for-aerosol-products">
  <h2><strong>R&D Formulation for Aerosol Products</strong></h2>
  <p>The R&D stage establishes the chemical and physical foundation of an aerosol product. At this level, small
            batches are used to identify the correct solvent system, emulsifier system (if applicable), propellant
            class, corrosion inhibition chemistry, and spray-performance targets. The objective is not production
            readiness but scientific feasibility and functional performance verification.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Small-scale screening of solvents, surfactants, and additives</li>
            <li>Preliminary propellant selection (LPG, DME as liquefied propellants; CO₂ and N₂ as compressed gases for
                pressure adjustment)</li>
            <li>Early spray testing using hand-filled cans</li>
            <li>Short-term stability checks for phase separation</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><strong>Typical Batch Sizes</strong></h3>
        <ul>
            <li>250 mL – 5 L liquid concentrate</li>
            <li>10–50 aerosol cans (hand-filled)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><strong>Equipment Used</strong></h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Laboratory stirrers</li>
            <li>Homogenizers</li>
            <li>pH meters and viscometers</li>
            <li>Bench-top crimpers and gassing heads</li>
        </ul>

